## Introduction
 includes the nice feature that it is possible to write your own objects into an output file which can then be read in a private analysis in the framework. A producer is a module that creates new objects and then puts the objects into the Event, LuminosityBlock, , or ProcessBlock. In this context, we usually call these objects products. These producer modules are called EDProducers. (EDFilters also have this ability, but except in rare cases it is better to use an EDProducer for this purpose). Output modules handle writing select products in Events, LuminosityBlocks, Runs, or ProcessBlocks to an output file.On this page we will only discuss how to produce objects that have a type that is already specially defined in the Framework for persistence. It is possible to create new persistent object types and write these into your events, but this is covered in [more advanced tutorials](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideCreatingNewProducts).
##  releases
This tutorial is updated to `CMSSW_13_3_1`.
## The basic principles and design concepts of EDProducers
[This is discussed here.](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/WorkBookCMSSWFramework#ComponentArch)
## the code
### Create the producer skeleton
The most convenient way to create code for a producer is by using the scripts that are available in the framework in FWCore/Skeletons. Alternately, you can create one from scratch or by copying then editing an existing producer similar to the new producer you want to create. To the skeleton script use the following steps:
```
cmsrel CMSSW_13_3_1
cd CMSSW_13_3_1/src/
cmsenv
git cms-addpkg PhysicsTools/PatExamples
```
Just for purposes of this example, this assumes you want to put the producer in the subsystem and package named PhysicsTools/PatExamples. You should select the actual subsystem and package based on the details of your producer. It is possible to create a new package and even a new subsystem, but describing that is beyond the scope of this page.
```
cd PhysicsTools
mkedprod TestProducer
cp TestProducer/plugins/TestProducer.cc PatExamples/plugins/.
rm -rf TestProducer
```
The `mkedprod` command above is the skeleton script that will create the file that will serve as a template to create a new EDProducer. This new file is just a starting point and will need to be edited. This script creates a whole directory structure with several files. This is useful if you are a new package along with the new EDProducer, but usually it is better to put the module into a package that already exists and already includes other modules. In that case, you are only interested in one or two of the files the script creates. The file TestProducer.cc contains the template for the module itself and should be copied to the existing package. You also might want to copy the file TestProducer/test/test\_catch2\_TestProducer.cc and use it as a starting point for a unit test for the new module before deleting the directory. There is additional documentation related to the skeleton code generators [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideSkeletonCodeGenerator).You might have to edit the existing BuildFile.xml file in the plugins directory, but often it is already written to build every file with a .cc suffix. If there are no new dependences, the module might build without any modifications to the BuildFile.xml file.
### Include the appropriate header files
You will get these header includes in the template file. Add additional ones as appropriate. In some cases, the header files , ParameterSet.h, StreamID.h or memory are not needed and can be deleted.
```
#include <memory>
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/Frameworkfwd.h"
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/stream/EDProducer.h"
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/"
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/MakerMacros.h"
#include "FWCore/ParameterSet/interface/ParameterSet.h"
#include "FWCore/Utilities/interface/StreamID.h"
```
### the class definition and add members
The class definition in the template will look like this.
```
class TestProducer : public edm::stream::EDProducer<> {
public:
  explicit TestProducer(const edm::ParameterSet&);
  ~TestProducer() override;
  static void fillDescriptions(edm::ConfigurationDescriptions& descriptions);
private:
  void beginStream(edm::StreamID) override;
  void produce(edm::Event&, const edm::EventSetup&) override;
  void endStream() override;
  //void :: const&, edm::EventSetup const&) override;
  //void endRun(edm:: const&, edm::EventSetup const&) override;
  //void beginLuminosityBlock(edm::LuminosityBlock const&, edm::EventSetup const&) override;
  //void endLuminosityBlock(edm::LuminosityBlock const&, edm::EventSetup const&) override;
  // ----------member data ---------------------------
};
```
The constructor and the produce function must be defined. Those two functions are required (even if they are empty). CMS strongly recommends implementing the fillDescriptions function. It will be used to validate the module configuration (details related to fillDescriptions are documented [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideConfigurationValidationAndHelp)). All the other functions are optional. If in your EDProducer one of the other functions does nothing, you should delete it entirely from the file. There are templates for the function definitions below the class definition.The file ends with the line that creates the plugin. The argument is the plugin name used in the configuration.
```
DEFINE_FWK_MODULE(TestProducer);
```
When editing the file delete any comments that are not useful. Many of them are there to explain where to put things as you edit the template, but are not useful in final EDProducer file. There is space for a one line description and some notes on the implementation details at the top.In the template, the class is always defined to be a `stream::EDProducer` with an empty template parameter list. There are other types of classes that have different properties related to multi threading. One can also add abilities to these classes by adding template parameters. This is for more advanced users. The following links describe this in detail with many examples:
* [summary of the different module types](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/FWMultithreadedFrameworkModuleTypes)
* [stream](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/FWMultithreadedFrameworkStreamModuleInterface)
* [global](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/FWMultithreadedFrameworkGlobalModuleInterface)
* [one](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/FWMultithreadedFrameworkOneModuleInterface)
### the class constructor
Add a put token as a data member of the EDProducer class definition.
```
class TestProducer {
  // ...
  edm::EDPutTokenT<SampleCollection> putToken_;
};
```
Then call the produces function in the constructor to declare the product your EDProducer produces.
```
TestProducer::TestProducer(edm::ParameterSet const& ps) :
  putToken_{produces()} {}
  // OR
  putToken_{produces("instanceName")} {}
```
The `produces` function tells the Framework that the module might put an object of type SampleCollection into the Event. This function must be called for every product that the producer might put in the Event. It can be called more than once. The producer might not actually put the object for some or all Events. If the produces function is not passed an argument it assumes the instance name is an empty string.There are more details related to this described [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideCreatingNewProducts#Producing_the_EDProduct). At this link one can see an older syntax for calling produces that still appears frequently in the code base. It describes setting a branch alias in ROOT. It also describes how a module helper class can call `produces`. The syntax if the product is put in a or LuminosityBlock is described [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuidePerRunAndPerLumiBlockData#Producing_an_EDProduct_and_p_AN1). One can also put products in a ProcessBlock.
#### the `produce()` method
In the `produce` method you will have to create the appropriate objects, fill them and put them in the event:
```
void TestProducer::produce(edm::Event& event, edm::EventSetup const&) {
   auto result = std::make_unique<SampleCollection>();
   // ... fill the collection ...
   event.put(putToken_, std::move(result));
}
```
The following code does almost exactly the same thing, but it is slightly better if the product type can be efficiently moved (all vectors are movable as well as many other types).
```
void TestProducer::produce(edm::Event& event, edm::EventSetup const&) {
  SampleCollection result;
  // ... fill the collection ...
  event.emplace(putToken_, std::move(result));
}
```
This syntax has worked since `CMSSW_12_1_0` and is recommended. An alternate older syntax that still works and appears often in  is described [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideCreatingNewProducts#Producing_the_EDProduct).An EDProducer can read data from the input to use while it is filling the objects it produces. Here is a simple example of how a module gets data from an Event:
```
// #include header with type definition for input data
class TestProducer : public edm::stream::EDProducer<> {
  public:
    explicit TestProducer(edm::ParameterSet const& pset)
        : token_(consumes(pset.getParameter<edm::InputTag>("inputTag"))),
          // ....
    void produce(edm::Event& event, edm::EventSetup const&) const {
      auto const& product = event.get(token_);
      // ...
    }
    static void fillDescriptions(edm::ConfigurationDescriptions& descriptions) {
      edm::ParameterSetDescription desc;
      desc.add<edm::InputTag>("inputTag");
      // ...
      descriptions.add("myLabelForCfi", desc);
    }
  private:
    const edm::EDGetTokenT<edmtest::IntProduct> token_;
    // ...
  };
```
There is more information related to getting data from the event [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideEDMGetDataFromEvent).An EDProducer can also get information from the EventSetup while filling the objects it produces. This is described in detail at these locations:
* [ESTokens and calling esConsumes from an EDProducer](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideHowToGetDataFromES#In_ED_module)
* [Getting data from EventSetup with a token](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideHowToGetDataFromES#Getting_data_from_EventSetup_wit)
#### Edit the `BuildFile`
If you place the new module in an existing package you might not need to edit the BuildFile.xml at all. You might need to add lines that declare the other packages your new module depends on. BuildFile's are discussed in more detail [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/WorkBookBuildFilesIntro).
#### And... Compile!
You are now ready to compile:
```
cd $CMSSW_BASE/src
scram b
```
### Running the producer
Here is an example module that includes the features discussed above all together. This module would iterate over tracks and copy the inner points into one collection and the outer points into another collection.
```
// -*- C++ -*-
//
// Package:    ExampleSubsystem/ExamplePackage
// Class:      TrackAndPointsProducer
//
/**\class TrackAndPointsProducer
 Description: Example module copies points to output
 Implementation:
     Loops over a track collection and copies the first
     and last Points to an output collection of points.
*/
//
// Original Author:  Sudhir_Malik
//                            (Major update, , Feb. 2024)
//         Created:  Tue Nov 18 17:42:49 CST 2008
#include <memory>
#include <vector>
#include "DataFormats/Math/interface/Point3D.h"
#include "DataFormats/TrackReco/interface/Track.h"
#include "DataFormats/TrackReco/interface/TrackFwd.h"
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/Frameworkfwd.h"
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/stream/EDProducer.h"
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/"
#include "FWCore/Framework/interface/MakerMacros.h"
#include "FWCore/ParameterSet/interface/ParameterSet.h"
#include "FWCore/Utilities/interface/InputTag.h"
class TrackAndPointsProducer : public edm::stream::EDProducer<> {
public:
  explicit TrackAndPointsProducer(edm::ParameterSet const&);
  static void fillDescriptions(edm::ConfigurationDescriptions&);
private:
  void produce(edm::Event&, const edm::EventSetup&) override;
  using Point = math::XYZPointD;
  using PointCollection = std::vector<Point>;
  const edm::EDGetTokenT<reco::TrackCollection> getToken_;
  edm::EDPutTokenT<PointCollection> innerPointsPutToken_;
  edm::EDPutTokenT<PointCollection> outerPointsPutToken_;
};
TrackAndPointsProducer::TrackAndPointsProducer(const edm::ParameterSet& pset)
    : getToken_(consumes(pset.getParameter<edm::InputTag>("src"))),
      innerPointsPutToken_{produces("innerPoint")},
      // If you want to call setBranchAlias, then provide a template argument to produces
      outerPointsPutToken_{produces<PointCollection>("outerPoint").setBranchAlias("outerPoints")} {}
void TrackAndPointsProducer::fillDescriptions(edm::ConfigurationDescriptions& descriptions) {
  edm::ParameterSetDescription desc;
  desc.add<edm::InputTag>("src");
  descriptions.add("trackAndPointsProducer", desc);
}
void TrackAndPointsProducer::produce(edm::Event& event, const edm::EventSetup& iSetup) {
  auto const& tracks = event.get(getToken_);
  auto innerPoints = std::make_unique<PointCollection>();
  auto outerPoints = std::make_unique<PointCollection>();
  const auto size = tracks.size();
  innerPoints->reserve(size);
  outerPoints->reserve(size);
  for (auto const& track : tracks) {
    innerPoints->push_back(track.innerPosition());
    outerPoints->push_back(track.outerPosition());
  }
  event.put(innerPointsPutToken_, std::move(innerPoints));
  event.put(outerPointsPutToken_, std::move(outerPoints));
  // Using emplace is a superior alternative for types that are efficiently
  // movable. A vector and many other types can be moved efficiently.
  /*
  PointCollection innerPoints;
  PointCollection outerPoints;
  const auto size = tracks.size();
  innerPoints.reserve(size);
  outerPoints.reserve(size);
  for (auto const& track : tracks) {
    innerPoints.push_back(track.innerPosition());
    outerPoints.push_back(track.outerPosition());
  }
  event.emplace(innerPointsPutToken_, std::move(innerPoints));
  event.emplace(outerPointsPutToken_, std::move(outerPoints));
  */
}
DEFINE_FWK_MODULE(TrackAndPointsProducer);
```
To this module you would need a configuration file that might look something like the example below. It includes
* Two instances of the producer module with different module labels. The `src` parameter specifies the track collection to use as input.
* An input file of some sorts. You can explore use the [Data Aggregation System (DAS) tool![](/twiki/pub/TWiki/TWikiDocGraphics/external-link.gif)](https://cmsweb.cern.ch/das/) to find data files you want to look at, or [create your own](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/WorkBookReco);
* An output module configured with an output filename and instructions on which products to keep or drop
Create a file that might be named trackandpointsproducer\_cfg.py.
```
import FWCore.ParameterSet. as cms
process = cms.Process("MYTESTPROCESS")
process.load("FWCore.MessageService.MessageLogger_cfi")
process.maxEvents = cms.untracked.PSet( input = cms.untracked.int32(100) )
process.source = cms.Source("PoolSource",
    # replace 'myfile.root' with the source file you want to use
    fileNames = cms.untracked.vstring(
       'file:myfile.root'
    )
)
# Note that the next part is often separated into a separate python file with the suffix _cfi.py
process.muonTrackPoints = cms.EDProducer('TrackAndPointsProducer'
        ,src = cms.InputTag('globalMuons')
)
process.trackTrackPoints = cms.EDProducer('TrackAndPointsProducer'
        ,src = cms.InputTag('generalTracks')
)
process.out = cms.OutputModule("PoolOutputModule",
    fileName = cms.untracked.string('myOutputFile.root'),
    outputCommands = cms.untracked.vstring('drop *',
        "keep *_generalTracks_*_*",
        "keep *_globalMuons_*_*",
        "keep *_muonTrackPoints_*_*",
        "keep *_trackTrackPoints_*_*")
)
# Usually it is better to put producers on a task instead of a path
# but paths also work.
process.p = cms.Path(process.muonTrackPoints*process.trackTrackPoints)
process.e = cms.EndPath(process.out)
```
There are a lot more details about writing configuration files [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideAboutPythonConfigFile).There is documentation related to selecting products for output [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/SWGuideSelectingBranchesForOutput).To the module do the following:
```
cmsRun trackandpointsproducer_cfg.py
```
This creates an output root file called `myOutputFile.root`
### Look at the output
If you want to see the products in an output file in the Events TTree, you can issue this command.
```
 file:myOutputFile.root
```
There is also a help option that will show other options for .
```
 --help
```
This is a ROOT file and the tools in ROOT can also be used to examine an output file, e.g. TBrowser. Find more info [here](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/WorkBookFWLite).(Note there are some pieces that prevent the above configuration from successfully running. PointCollection is not defined as persistent in  and an input file holding the correctly named TrackCollection would need to be found. If someone wanted to invest a little time and fix those shortcomings it might successfully execute...)
## Example of a Simple Producer ([EventCountProducer](/twiki/bin/edit/CMSPublic/EventCountProducer?topicparent=CMSPublic.WorkBookEDMTutorialProducer;nowysiwyg=1 "this topic does not yet exist; you can create it."))
In many workflows, you might a filter that drops some portion of events, but you want to keep track of how many events were present before the filter was . This information can be provided by a simple tool called [EventCountProducer![](/twiki/pub/TWiki/TWikiDocGraphics/external-link.gif)](https://cmssdt.cern.ch/dxr//source/CommonTools/UtilAlgos/plugins/EventCountProducer.cc). (This is a somewhat simplistic example that illustrates how this might be done. Realistic use cases don't actually use EventCountProducer.)In your python configuration, you simply create an instance and then include it in your path at the point where you want to count events. For example, if you include a filter to select events with muons, you could create two producers, one to count events before the filter and one to count the number of events that pass the filter:
```
process.nEventsTotal = cms.EDProducer("EventCountProducer")
process.nEventsFiltered = cms.EDProducer("EventCountProducer")
process.p = cms.Path(
    process.nEventsTotal *
    process.muonFilter *
    process.nEventsFiltered
)
```
The EventCountProducer stores its product in the luminosity block and is able to merge event counts from multiple files. So, if you were patTuples in the previous step, and were now running an analyzer on several patTuple files, you could access the event counts in the `endLuminosityBlock` method of your analyzer:
```
void MyAnalyzer::endLuminosityBlock(const edm::LuminosityBlock & lumi, const EventSetup & setup) {
  // Total number of events is the sum of the events in each of these luminosity blocks
  auto const& nEventsTotalCounter = lumi.get(totalToken_);
  nEventsTotal += nEventsTotalCounter.value;
  auto const& nEventsFilteredCounter = lumi.get(filteredToken_);
  nEventsFiltered += nEventsFilteredCounter.value;
}
```
## Summary and Conclusion
This tutorial shows how to implement a producer. Within the same process, producers allow one module to use the output of another module. Producers are also necessary for writing products to output files (output modules are also required). This enables the results of the modules in a process to be reused. [EDM](/twiki/bin//CMSPublic/EDM) files allow subsequent processes to take advantage of all the features associated with the Framework and in many use cases this is superior to standalone `ntuple` formats and other formats. For example, debugging and data management are often easier with jobs with the Framework. In some circumstances performance is better and the Framework supports multi-threading.
